TimescaleDB (container image only, not source)

DeviceChain publishes a PostgreSQL container image carrying the TimescaleDB
extension, used by the time-series event store (ADR-004, ADR-020):

  ghcr.io/devicechain-io/postgresql-timescaledb

  TimescaleDB — https://github.com/timescale/timescaledb
  Copyright (c) Timescale, Inc.
  TimescaleDB Community Edition, licensed under the Timescale License (TSL).

The TSL is a source-available license and is NOT an open source license. It is
NOT compatible with the Apache License, Version 2.0, and this component is
therefore not offered under Apache-2.0.

Scope of this notice — what is and is not affected:

  * The DeviceChain SOURCE in this repository remains Apache-2.0 in its
    entirety. The files that produce the image (deploy/images/timescaledb/)
    are DeviceChain's own Apache-2.0 sources and contain no Timescale code;
    they install a published package. No DeviceChain source is a derivative
    work of TimescaleDB.
  * The obligation attaches to the published IMAGE, which redistributes
    TimescaleDB in binary form. The TSL permits that redistribution. The image
    carries, and its build asserts the presence of:

      /usr/share/licenses/timescaledb/LICENSE-TIMESCALE
          the full text of the Timescale License Agreement, fetched at build
          time from the upstream tag matching the version installed.
      /usr/share/licenses/timescaledb/copyright
          the Debian package's DEP-5 per-file licence map. Note this file is
          NOT the licence: its `Files: *` stanza names Apache-2.0 and its
          `tsl/*` stanza only links to the Timescale License.

    The image also carries an OCI description recording that it contains
    TSL-licensed code.

This is not a new dependency. DeviceChain has always run TimescaleDB Community
Edition — the upstream `timescale/timescaledb` image is that same TSL build.
Continuous aggregates, compression, retention policies and the background job
scheduler are TSL-only features, and the data-lifecycle design (ADR-026) uses
all four; the Apache-2 edition of TimescaleDB does not provide them.

Operators and downstream distributors should note the TSL's principal
restriction: TimescaleDB may not be used to offer a database-as-a-service, and
DeviceChain must not expose the underlying Timescale instance to tenants as a
general-purpose database service. DeviceChain uses it as an internal store
behind its own APIs, which is the "value added product" use the TSL permits.
